        "**RQ2 Findings:**\n",
        "\n",
        "The results did not support our hypothesis, as mixed-breed dogs are adopted at *higher* rates than purebred dogs in every city. In Austin, 46% of mixed-breed dogs are adopted versus 33% of purebred dogs. Louisville shows a similar pattern (40% vs. 33%). Sonoma and Bloomington follow the same direction, with mixed-breed adoption rates of 28% and 59% compared to purebred rates of 18% and 53%, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "Chi-square tests confirm that the association between breed type and adoption is statistically significant in all four cities (all *p* < 0.001). This means the difference is unlikely due to chance. One major reason why the results differed from our hypothesis is that mixed-breed dogs greatly outnumber purebred dogs in the shelters we examined. It is also possible that adopters visiting shelters are specifically seeking mixed-breed dogs rather than going to breeders for purebreds, though it is more likely the former reason.\n",
        "\n",
