        "**RQ3 Findings:**\n",
        "\n",
        "Across all four cities, dogs that were adopted had a *longer* median length of stay than dogs that were not adopted. In Austin, adopted dogs had a median stay of 7 days versus 3 days for non-adopted dogs. Louisville showed a similar pattern (10 vs. 5 days). Sonoma had the largest gap (28 vs. 3 days), and Bloomington fell in between (19 vs. 8 days). Mann-Whitney U tests confirmed that these distributional differences are statistically significant in all four cities (all *p* < 0.001).\n",
        "\n",
        "This result runs in opposition to our original hypothesis that longer stays reduce adoption probability. Instead, the pattern reflects a confound: many non-adopted dogs exit the shelter very quickly through euthanasia or transfer, which actually shortens their length of stay. Adopted dogs, on the other hand, need time in the shelter to be seen and selected by adopters. In other words, a short stay does not mean a dog was easy to adopt. Rather, it may mean the dog was removed from the shelter through a non-adoption pathway before it had the chance.\n",
        "\n",
        "This finding highlights a major limitation of interpreting length of stay as a straightforward predictor. Dogs may be held longer *because* shelters are actively trying to place them, while sadly, dogs deemed less adoptable may be euthanized or transferred sooner. The logistic regression in the full analysis will control for age and breed to help isolate the independent effect of LOS."
      ]
